-The remaining API, recovery and deployment sections are completed with the
-N1 integration and delivery tasks.
+Versions and activation boundary
+--------------------------------
+
+This fork reserves Compute API microversion ``2.106``, compute RPC ``6.5``,
+compute-task RPC ``1.26`` and service version ``69``. The general conductor
+RPC stays at ``3.0`` and the ordinary Instance object at ``2.8``. These private
+numbers do not imply compatibility with another fork using the same numbers.
+Mixed versions and a missing or unsupported live probe deny admission before
+any checkpoint effect. A positive version check alone is insufficient.
+
+The intended N2 profile is ``kvm-bios-same-host-v1``: KVM/libvirt, BIOS and the
+same compute, with every writable disk backed by Cinder. Ephemeral and swap
+disks, shares, multiattach, passthrough, vTPM and confidential VMs are outside
+this profile. Unknown properties must not be treated as supported. N2 must
+validate the actual domain, device topology, backend capabilities and runtime
+key material before destructive effects. N1 only validates its DB projection.
+It never registers the test executor as a production driver.
+
+Schema rollout precedes activation:
+
+#. Keep new checkpoint creation disabled. Upgrade the API DB through migration
+   ``71be90d2c402`` using the normal ``nova-manage api_db sync`` procedure.
+#. Upgrade every cell DB, including cell0, through ``4a8c2d7e9101`` using
+   ``nova-manage db sync`` with that cell's database configuration.
+#. Upgrade every API, conductor, compute and other DB writer. Old writers do
+   not participate in the guards. This is not a mixed-writer activation mode.
+#. Before any future activation, complete N2/backend validation, credential
+   design and runtime fault testing; configure all writers consistently.
+
+This sequence describes schema prerequisites, not a claim that N1 can be
+activated for production RAM checkpoints. Do not run these commands against
+an operating cloud merely to reproduce the local N1 test suite.
+
+The ``[checkpoint]`` defaults are::
+
+    enabled = false
+    max_checkpoints_per_instance = 8
+    max_checkpoints_per_project = 100
+    max_artifact_bytes_per_project = 0
+    reconcile_interval = 60
+
+Zero bytes denies new creates; it does not mean unlimited. Negative limits are
+invalid. Pending and retained checkpoints and resources awaiting cleanup all
+consume the count and byte reservations. The byte limit includes staging.
+This ledger is separate from ordinary Nova quotas and spans all project cells.
+
+Admission and recovery
+----------------------
+
+The API DB stores project usage and quota tickets. Each cell stores checkpoint,
+disk, artifact, operation, step, guard and admission-cancellation records.
+There is no transaction spanning the API and cell databases. Create reserves
+a global ticket, admits under the Instance lock, confirms that same ticket,
+marks the cell operation quota-ready, then dispatches. A step cannot be claimed
+before quota readiness. Request UUID reuse is scoped to project and server;
+the actor, action and canonical payload must also match. Authorization is
+checked again on every replay.
+
+Cancellation writes a durable cell tombstone before refunding the ticket.
+Late admission of that operation is then fenced. Delete and completed create
+cleanup release usage only after a durable cell proof that all owned resources
+are gone. A timeout, missing reply, down cell or one ``operation not found``
+response never refunds a ticket. Successful create holds its ticket until
+deletion. Byte growth reserves globally before writing; shrink records a
+versioned cell proof before reducing the global charge.
+
+Each backend intent receives a stable UUID before I/O. Once claimed, an
+uncertain step is queried by that UUID, never blindly executed again.
+Conductor startup and bounded periodic passes reconcile quota and local-cell
+journals independently, even when creation is disabled. Unclaimed subsequent
+steps can proceed only after the previous result is proven. Incomplete revert
+or unknown runtime effects retain ownership and block automatic guest start,
+power synchronization and conflicting callbacks. A restart does not grant
+takeover. Generation remains 1 in N1.
+
+Runtime-save evidence and published artifacts must identify the same objects,
+including kind, logical identifier, checksum and bytes. Two live points on
+the same VM cannot reuse a raw artifact or snapshot identifier. N2 additionally
+owns namespace isolation across VMs/cells and real backend retention semantics.
+Deleting A must not remove B's data.
+
+The requesting user's UUID is audit identity, not a credential. N1 does not
+persist bearer tokens or recover an expired user's token. Recovery creates a
+service context for internal journal work; it does not impersonate the Cinder
+C1 owner. N2 must define and validate background Cinder authorization. Current
+Neutron port identity is checked, but IP leases, security groups and external
+network state are not rolled back. SAN, libvirt and RabbitMQ behavior requires
+separate runtime evidence.
+
+Read-only diagnosis
+-------------------
+
+Use ``GET /servers/{server_id}/checkpoint-capabilities`` at microversion
+``2.106`` to inspect support and closed reason codes. Default N1 returns
+unsupported. Project readers can list/show checkpoints and inspect
+``GET /servers/{server_id}/checkpoint-operations/{operation_id}``; project
+members can request create/revert/delete. System scope and another project's
+server are not an authorization shortcut. Public responses omit host, paths,
+tokens, executor internals and per-disk backend evidence.
+
+After a ``202`` response, poll the operation resource. The acceptance projection
+and checkpoint projection can be observed at different instants: completion
+can precede the HTTP response. ``recovery_required`` means inspect the journal,
+not retry the backend manually. For example, with an existing authenticated
+token and server UUID::
+
+    curl -H "X-Auth-Token: $OS_TOKEN" \
+      -H "OpenStack-API-Version: compute 2.106" \
+      "$COMPUTE_ENDPOINT/servers/$SERVER_ID/checkpoint-capabilities"
+
+Operators can correlate the operation UUID, phase and closed error code with
+read-only cell journal records and the original API DB ticket. Check the
+Instance guard, step status/evidence, quota readiness and release proof before
+diagnosing a stranded operation. Treat internal tokens and resource locations
+as sensitive; do not copy raw journal rows into public API responses or logs.
+Ordinary queued/running claims with unknown outcomes are quarantined for
+investigation. N1 intentionally has no force-unlock, expiry, SQL repair command
+or automatic ordinary-worker takeover. Do not clear task state or delete
+ownership/quota rows to make an operation appear finished.
+
+The normal archive/purge path retains durable checkpoint history, including
+deleted/tombstone records. Tests use two SQL cells, actual WSGI/RPC serializers,
+MySQL/PostgreSQL lock races and test-only effects. They establish the N1
+protocol, not live Keystone, hypervisor, storage or network readiness.
